{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# ML-06 — Signal Audit: Do the Flags Hold?\n",
    "\n",
    "[![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/Di-pesh/flyinterm/blob/main/work/notebooks/w04_signal_audit.ipynb)\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook checks whether the intuition behind a content-refresh flag actually shows up in the data. The bar is intentionally high: a signal is only useful if the direction is clear, the sample is large enough, and the business rule has a visible lift against the label.\n",
    "\n",
    "The label source is `trend_direction`, which is derived from `trend_pct`, so this notebook uses it only for audit checks and never as a feature."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from pathlib import Path\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "\n",
    "def find_data_file():\n",
    "    root = Path.cwd().resolve()\n",
    "    for p in [root, *root.parents]:\n",
    "        candidate = p / 'data' / 'raw' / 'content_refresh_anonymized.csv'\n",
    "        if candidate.exists():\n",
    "            return candidate\n",
    "    raise FileNotFoundError('Expected data/raw/content_refresh_anonymized.csv')\n",
    "\n",
    "raw_path = find_data_file()\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv(raw_path).drop_duplicates(subset='content_id').copy()\n",
    "df['avg_position_clean'] = df['avg_position'].replace(0, np.nan)\n",
    "df['is_declining'] = df['trend_direction'].astype(str).str.lower().eq('down').astype(int)\n",
    "\n",
    "numeric_fields = ['impressions_90d', 'clicks_90d', 'sessions_90d', 'avg_position_clean', 'content_age_days', 'trend_pct']\n",
    "summary = df[numeric_fields].describe().T\n",
    "print('Numeric summary (all rows):')\n",
    "print(summary.round(3).to_string())\n",
    "print('\nTop missingness:')\n",
    "print(df.isna().sum().sort_values(ascending=False).head(10).to_string())\n",
    "print(f'\nRows used: {len(df):,}; decline base rate: {df[\'is_declining\'].mean():.3f}')\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Signal test #1 / #2 / #3 with verdicts\n",
    "\n",
    "Each check below follows the same pattern: bucket the feature, show `n` in each bucket, compare decline rates, and assign a verdict only when the pattern is large enough to be credible. The test is intentionally simple and interpretable rather than over-optimized."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def signal_table(series, label, n_bins=5):\n",
    "    cleaned = series.replace(0, np.nan)\n",
    "    if cleaned.dropna().nunique() < 2:\n",
    "        print(f'\n{label}: insufficient variation for a bucketed test.')\n",
    "        return None\n",
    "    bins = pd.qcut(cleaned, q=n_bins, duplicates='drop')\n",
    "    out = df.assign(_bucket=bins).groupby('_bucket', dropna=False).agg(\n",
    "        n=('is_declining', 'size'),\n",
    "        decline_rate=('is_declining', 'mean')\n",
    "    ).reset_index()\n",
    "    out['bucket'] = out['_bucket'].astype(str)\n",
    "    out = out[['bucket', 'n', 'decline_rate']].copy()\n",
    "    out['decline_rate'] = out['decline_rate'].round(3)\n",
    "    print(f'\n{label}')\n",
    "    print(out.to_string(index=False))\n",
    "    return out\n",
    "\n",
    "signal_1 = signal_table(df['avg_position_clean'], 'Test 1: avg_position vs decline rate')\n",
    "signal_2 = signal_table(df['content_age_days'], 'Test 2: content_age_days vs decline rate')\n",
    "signal_3 = signal_table(df['impressions_90d'], 'Test 3: impressions_90d vs decline rate')\n",
    "\n",
    "def verdict_from_table(table, direction):\n",
    "    if table is None:\n",
    "        return 'INSUFFICIENT DATA'\n",
    "    rates = table['decline_rate'].to_numpy()\n",
    "    if rates.size < 2:\n",
    "        return 'INSUFFICIENT DATA'\n",
    "    if direction == 'up':\n",
    "        return 'CONFIRMED' if np.nanmax(rates) > np.nanmin(rates) and np.nanmean(np.diff(rates)) > 0 else 'MIXED'\n",
    "    if direction == 'down':\n",
    "        return 'CONFIRMED' if np.nanmax(rates) > np.nanmin(rates) and np.nanmean(np.diff(rates)) < 0 else 'MIXED'\n",
    "    return 'INSUFFICIENT DATA'\n",
    "\n",
    "print('Verdicts:')\n",
    "print(f'1. avg_position: {verdict_from_table(signal_1, \'up\')}')\n",
    "print(f'2. content_age_days: {verdict_from_table(signal_2, \'up\')}')\n",
    "print(f'3. impressions_90d: {verdict_from_table(signal_3, \'up\')}')\n",
    "print('Interpretation: use the direction and bucket sizes together; a tiny group with a huge ratio is not enough evidence.')\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. The flag-linked test\n",
    "\n",
    "A product rule is only defensible when it visibly separates the decline rate in the data. Here the rule says: 'pages with average position worse than the median are more likely to decline.' We test that assumption directly and print `n` to avoid making a claim from a small slice."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "median_position = df['avg_position_clean'].median()\n",
    "flagged = df['avg_position_clean'] > median_position\n",
    "flag_table = df.assign(flagged=flagged).groupby('flagged')['is_declining'].agg(['mean', 'size']).reset_index()\n",
    "flag_table = flag_table.rename(columns={'mean': 'decline_rate', 'size': 'n'})\n",
    "flag_table['flagged'] = flag_table['flagged'].map({True: 'weak_position', False: 'strong_position'})\n",
    "print(f'Median avg_position (excluding 0=no data): {median_position:.3f}')\n",
    "print(flag_table.to_string(index=False))\n",
    "\n",
    "flagged_rate = df.loc[flagged, 'is_declining'].mean()\n",
    "unflagged_rate = df.loc[~flagged, 'is_declining'].mean()\n",
    "print(f'Decline rate among weak-position pages: {flagged_rate:.3f}')\n",
    "print(f'Decline rate among stronger-position pages: {unflagged_rate:.3f}')\n",
    "if flagged_rate > unflagged_rate and min(flag_table['n']) >= 50:\n",
    "    print('The flag is supported by the data: weaker position is associated with a higher decline rate.')\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    print('The flag is not strongly supported by this slice; we should not treat it as a reliable production rule without more audit.')\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. What this means in practice\n",
    "\n",
    "A flag only matters when it survives a direct signal check. If the data does not show a consistent relationship, the right conclusion is not 'we need a bigger model'; it is 'we should not rely on that rule yet.'"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print('Audit complete: inspect the directional relationship, not just raw rank ordering.')\n",
    "print(f'Observed decline base rate: {df[\'is_declining\'].mean():.3f}')\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Self-check\n",
    "\n",
    "- [x] The notebook inspects distributions before making a decision.\n",
    "- [x] Three signal checks are included with a verdict framework and `n` counts.\n",
    "- [x] A flag-linked rule is tested against the label using a direct comparison.\n",
    "- [x] Missing/zero-handling is explicit (`avg_position = 0` means no data).\n",
    "- [x] The notebook avoids treating label-derived columns as features."
   ]
  }
